\description{
Is the index's recent volatility normal? The analysis looks at
the daily log returns, \code{log(close(t) / close(t-1))}, from the close of one
trading day to the next, in two ways. All the windows count trading days,
and a year is 252 of them.

\strong{The last month} (\code{month_days} trading days, 21 by default): its
volatility, the standard deviation of the daily returns times \code{sqrt(252)},
against the year before
it and against every past period of the same length; an F test and the
Fligner-Killeen test, which is robust to a few extreme days, of whether
its variance is larger than the year before; its largest daily move, in
standard deviations of the year before; and its return against every past
period of the same length.

\strong{The last days} (\code{recent_days} days): their return against every past
period of as many days; the same, in standard deviations of the year
before; a GARCH(1,1) model with Student-t errors (see \code{\link[=garch_fit]{garch_fit()}}),
fitted to the \code{garch_years} years before them, which judges each day
against the volatility the model expected for it given the days before;
and each day's volume against the \code{volume_days} days before it (see
\code{\link[=volume_ratio]{volume_ratio()}}), judged by how often the index's volume was as far from
usual.

\strong{The next days} (\code{forecast_days} days): the same model, with its
variance brought up to the last day, forecasts the volatility and the range
of the price (see \code{\link[=garch_forecast]{garch_forecast()}}), and gives the value at risk and the
expected shortfall of the next day (see \code{\link[=garch_risk]{garch_risk()}}). A Kupiec test (see
\code{\link[=kupiec_test]{kupiec_test()}}) checks how often the model's value at risk was exceeded
over the last year. The value at risk and expected shortfall of the last
year's daily returns are given without the model too.
